WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:04.794
<v Maya>Every time a major piece of new technology arrives on the scene, we tend to panic a bit.</v>

2
00:00:05.054 --> 00:00:09.317
<v Daniel>Oh, absolutely. We always assume the machines are finally going to just permanently wipe out human jobs.</v>

3
00:00:09.497 --> 00:00:19.243
<v Maya>Right. But here we are in the 21st century surrounded by unprecedented automation. And, you know, there are actually more jobs than ever. It's this massive counterintuitive paradox.</v>

4
00:00:19.795 --> 00:00:28.662
<v Daniel>It completely defies our basic intuition about how the economy actually works. I mean, for over a century, economists have been fighting this thing called the lump of labor fallacy.</v>

5
00:00:28.682 --> 00:00:33.785
<v Maya>The lump of labor. That was David Schloss back in 1891, right?</v>

6
00:00:34.146 --> 00:00:41.551
<v Daniel>Exactly. It's this deeply ingrained but totally false assumption that there is only a fixed, finite amount of work to go around.</v>

7
00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:50.370
<v Maya>I always picture that like a fixed pie. People get terrified that if a machine eats a slice of the pie, there is just permanently less pie for the humans.</v>

8
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:59.418
<v Daniel>That is the perfect way to visualize it. But looking at the historical data, technology doesn't just eat the pie. It usually, well, it builds a much bigger bakery.</v>

9
00:00:59.677 --> 00:01:10.399
<v Maya>Right. It invents entirely new pastries we didn't even know we wanted. So welcome to this deep dive. We know you are looking for hard, empirical evidence about the future of work, especially in higher education.</v>

10
00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:13.580
<v Daniel>You need actionable data, right? Stuff that cuts through the noise.</v>

11
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:27.703
<v Maya>You need the numbers to explain exactly how technology reshapes labor. So today we are answering one central question. When technology takes tasks away, what exactly does it replace them with? And why is that new work fundamentally different?</v>

12
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:41.462
<v Daniel>To really unpack that, we are grounding our discussion in a landmark 2026 paper by David Autor, Caroline Chin, Anna Solomons, and Brian Siegmiller. It's titled, What Makes New Work Different from More Work?</v>

13
00:01:42.103 --> 00:01:48.084
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this, because to understand their breakthrough, we kind of have to start by acknowledging the harsh reality of right now.</v>

14
00:01:48.604 --> 00:01:55.445
<v Daniel>Yeah, the optimistic bakery metaphor is true over the long run, but in the short term, the threat of displacement is incredibly real.</v>

15
00:01:56.036 --> 00:02:01.441
<v Maya>So the pie might be getting bigger eventually, but the current workers aren't necessarily getting a slice today.</v>

16
00:02:01.822 --> 00:02:13.313
<v Daniel>Precisely. I mean, the labor share of income in the U.S., which is the slice of the total economic pie that goes to workers' wages instead of corporate profits, that has fallen about 10 percent since the year 2000.</v>

17
00:02:14.298 --> 00:02:15.598
<v Maya>10% is massive.</v>

18
00:02:15.998 --> 00:02:26.680
<v Daniel>It really is. And task models of automation actually predict this. They predict that machines will just keep narrowing the variety of human work until, well, until there's practically nothing left.</v>

19
00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:36.422
<v Maya>And we have historical precedent for how brutal that narrowing can be, right? In the background research for today, there's this fascinating 2024 paper by Feigenbaum and Gross looking at AT&T.</v>

20
00:02:36.842 --> 00:02:40.223
<v Daniel>Oh, the mechanical switching study. That one is incredible.</v>

21
00:02:40.457 --> 00:02:51.922
<v Maya>Yeah. So between 1920 and 1940, AT&T introduced mechanical switching to the phone network. Before that, you literally had to speak to a human operator who would plug a physical wire into a board to connect your call. Right.</v>

22
00:02:52.042 --> 00:02:53.302
<v Daniel>A highly manual process.</v>

23
00:02:53.462 --> 00:03:02.226
<v Maya>Exactly. And when they automated that, they wiped out 50 to 80 percent of local telephone operator jobs as cities converted. Just gone.</v>

24
00:03:02.926 --> 00:03:10.089
<v Daniel>And the fallout for those specific workers was devastating. The incumbent operators were mostly young women who really relied on those wages.</v>

25
00:03:10.619 --> 00:03:12.380
<v Maya>What happened to them after the machines took over?</v>

26
00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:25.143
<v Daniel>Well, a decade after the automation rolled out, those specific women were far more likely to be stuck in lower paying jobs or just completely pushed out of the workforce entirely compared to similar workers in cities that hadn't automated yet.</v>

27
00:03:25.583 --> 00:03:32.845
<v Maya>See, this is the exact demographic a system like CUNY serves today. Working class, incumbent workers who are just trying to build a stable life.</v>

28
00:03:33.165 --> 00:03:38.487
<v Daniel>Right. And if their specific daily task gets automated, they don't just magically bounce back into a better job.</v>

29
00:03:39.068 --> 00:03:48.375
<v Maya>But wait, here is the paradox we promised to solve. If AT&T wiped out all those jobs, why didn't the national unemployment rate just stay permanently elevated?</v>

30
00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:58.703
<v Daniel>Because of what economists call the reinstatement effect. If a machine takes a task away, the economy eventually reinstates labor by creating entirely new tasks.</v>

31
00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.761
<v Maya>We just call this new work, right?</v>

32
00:04:01.041 --> 00:04:14.827
<v Daniel>Exactly. Jeffrey Lynn really pioneered the measurement of this concept back in 2011. He showed that while AT&T was cutting switchboard operators, subsequent cohorts of workers were shifting into entirely new clerical and service categories.</v>

33
00:04:15.508 --> 00:04:18.409
<v Maya>Roles that simply hadn't existed before the technology came along.</v>

34
00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:29.860
<v Daniel>Exactly. Which brings us to the core claim of this new 2026 Otter paper. They argue that new work doesn't just matter because it creates a higher volume of tasks for people to do.</v>

35
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.920
<v Maya>OK, so why does it matter?</v>

36
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.582
<v Daniel>It matters because it demands novel, scarce expertise.</v>

37
00:04:37.182 --> 00:04:45.805
<v Maya>Novel, scarce expertise. So it's not just, you know, moving boxes from point A to point B in a slightly different automated warehouse.</v>

38
00:04:46.378 --> 00:04:58.207
<v Daniel>No, it's learning a completely new, highly specific way of operating that didn't exist yesterday. But to prove that new work actually requires scarce expertise, the researchers hit a massive roadblock.</v>

39
00:04:58.708 --> 00:05:04.152
<v Maya>Because they had to figure out how to measure something that by definition did not previously exist.</v>

40
00:05:04.452 --> 00:05:09.296
<v Daniel>Right. You can't just look at a standard economic chart if the job title wasn't even invented yet.</v>

41
00:05:09.717 --> 00:05:13.820
<v Maya>I am so fascinated by their methodology here. How do you measure the invisible?</v>

42
00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:24.899
<v Daniel>Historically, economists just had to guess. They used imputation methods where they would look at broad categories of employment and make rough assumptions about what percentage might be doing new things.</v>

43
00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.401
<v Maya>But Otter and his team wanted raw proof.</v>

44
00:05:27.702 --> 00:05:37.110
<v Daniel>Yes. They went straight to the census alphabetical index of industries and occupations. They tracked exactly when new official job titles were added to the index.</v>

45
00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:40.413
<v Maya>And then what? How do they connect that to real people?</v>

46
00:05:41.047 --> 00:05:48.510
<v Daniel>They matched those official new titles to verbatim, handwritten job write-ins from the 1940 and 1950 census complete count files.</v>

47
00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:56.634
<v Maya>Wait, they actually read the millions of individual handwritten responses where regular people described what they did for a living.</v>

48
00:05:57.014 --> 00:06:09.039
<v Daniel>Yeah, they used advanced data matching to link what people scribbled on their census forms decades ago to the emergence of these new official titles. And they did the same thing with Confidential American Community Survey data running all the way up to 2023.</v>

49
00:06:10.157 --> 00:06:15.218
<v Maya>Okay, I have to push back on this a little bit because there is a glaring risk of title inflation here.</v>

50
00:06:15.558 --> 00:06:16.378
<v Daniel>Title inflation.</v>

51
00:06:16.918 --> 00:06:31.161
<v Maya>Let's say I call myself a cybersecurity analyst on my census form in 2018. How do we know I'm actually doing novel work requiring scarce expertise and I'm not just a modern security guard who happens to sit at a keyboard?</v>

52
00:06:31.621 --> 00:06:38.663
<v Daniel>That is a very fair point. How do you separate genuine technological shift from a fancy new corporate buzzword?</v>

53
00:06:39.143 --> 00:06:39.903
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

54
00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:50.214
<v Daniel>No, the researchers actually anticipated that exact critique. To solve it, they used a large language model, GPT-4.0, to classify these new titles.</v>

55
00:06:50.594 --> 00:06:53.295
<v Maya>They used an AI to classify the data.</v>

56
00:06:53.415 --> 00:07:02.519
<v Daniel>They did. But they didn't just ask the AI to guess blindly. They fitted heavy historical context, economic definitions, and patent data.</v>

57
00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:07.721
<v Maya>So the AI was cross-referencing the job titles with actual technological patents?</v>

58
00:07:08.105 --> 00:07:14.569
<v Daniel>Yes. The AI's job was to determine if a title's emergence was fundamentally linked to a real technological change.</v>

59
00:07:15.049 --> 00:07:16.990
<v Maya>Give me an example of how that worked in practice.</v>

60
00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:26.862
<v Daniel>Okay, take a title like drama therapist. The AI looked at the historical data and determined that role was driven by cultural shifts in healthcare, not a new technology.</v>

61
00:07:27.322 --> 00:07:27.903
<v Maya>That makes sense.</v>

62
00:07:28.283 --> 00:07:38.729
<v Daniel>But for an artificial intelligence specialist, which first appeared in the index back in the year 2000, the AI saw the direct linkage to new software patents and processing capabilities.</v>

63
00:07:39.349 --> 00:07:46.413
<v Maya>Still, letting an AI make complex economic classification sounds a little risky. Did they verify the machine's work?</v>

64
00:07:46.648 --> 00:07:54.151
<v Daniel>They did. They conducted a really rigorous human audit, and the AI achieved a 95% agreement rate with human economists.</v>

65
00:07:54.291 --> 00:07:57.152
<v Maya>Wow, 95%. Yeah. That's incredibly high.</v>

66
00:07:57.412 --> 00:08:05.615
<v Daniel>It is. So they were able to confidently split the data, and they found that just over half of all new work is directly technology linked.</v>

67
00:08:06.275 --> 00:08:13.018
<v Maya>I want to add some vital nuance here from another piece of our background research. There's a 2020 paper by Adelaide and his colleagues.</v>

68
00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:14.790
<v Daniel>The newspaper ad study.</v>

69
00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:24.195
<v Maya>Yes. They looked at decades of newspaper help wanted ads and found that the actual daily tasks workers perform changed dramatically inside existing job titles.</v>

70
00:08:24.656 --> 00:08:31.920
<v Daniel>Right. So a secretary in 1950 was doing vastly different physical and cognitive tasks than a secretary in 1990.</v>

71
00:08:32.658 --> 00:08:43.447
<v Maya>Exactly. Even though the job title never changed once. Because of that, relying solely on brand new job titles might actually severely understate how much the nature of work is truly changing.</v>

72
00:08:43.868 --> 00:08:49.512
<v Daniel>That is a crucial caveat for you to remember. The official new titles are just the most visible tip of the iceberg.</v>

73
00:08:49.953 --> 00:08:59.040
<v Maya>But even just looking at that tip, the sheer volume of transformation is staggering. If there is a single number you need to take away to understand the scale of this, it's this.</v>

74
00:08:59.301 --> 00:08:59.701
<v Daniel>Let's hear it.</v>

75
00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:07.754
<v Maya>Today, nearly one in five workers, 18.3% to be exact, holds a job title that was introduced since 1970.</v>

76
00:09:08.514 --> 00:09:16.517
<v Daniel>Just to put that in historical perspective, back in 1950, only 7.2% of workers held titles that were new since 1930.</v>

77
00:09:17.057 --> 00:09:20.118
<v Maya>So the pace of new work creation has accelerated massively.</v>

78
00:09:20.516 --> 00:09:27.941
<v Daniel>It has, though we should subject that to some scrutiny. The strongest objection to that 18% figure is actually pretty interesting.</v>

79
00:09:28.201 --> 00:09:28.982
<v Maya>What's the objection?</v>

80
00:09:29.422 --> 00:09:30.903
<v Daniel>It's actually a downward revision.</v>

81
00:09:31.304 --> 00:09:31.464
<v Maya>Oh.</v>

82
00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:38.969
<v Daniel>The older imputation methods I mentioned earlier, the ones that relied on guessing, they claimed that about one in three jobs were new.</v>

83
00:09:39.309 --> 00:09:44.293
<v Maya>Oh, wow. So direct measurement brought the estimate down from a third to about a fifth.</v>

84
00:09:44.748 --> 00:09:51.890
<v Daniel>Exactly. But honestly, the more pressing concern isn't the total volume of new jobs. It is where these new jobs are landing.</v>

85
00:09:52.651 --> 00:09:53.151
<v Maya>What do you mean?</v>

86
00:09:53.531 --> 00:10:01.394
<v Daniel>Well, in 1950, new work was heavily concentrated in middle paid production and clerical jobs. It basically built the middle class.</v>

87
00:10:02.094 --> 00:10:02.534
<v Maya>And today?</v>

88
00:10:03.034 --> 00:10:06.735
<v Daniel>Today, new work has completely hollowed out the middle. It is bifurcated.</v>

89
00:10:07.075 --> 00:10:09.096
<v Maya>Bifurcated how? Where did the jobs go?</v>

90
00:10:09.556 --> 00:10:14.498
<v Daniel>The vast majority of new work is now concentrated at the very top in professional and managerial roles.</v>

91
00:10:15.237 --> 00:10:17.299
<v Maya>And for the folks who don't have a college degree.</v>

92
00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:24.687
<v Daniel>For non-college workers, new work has shifted almost entirely toward the bottom into much lower paying health and personal services.</v>

93
00:10:25.148 --> 00:10:31.935
<v Maya>Wow. So if the high-end new work has migrated to professional and managerial roles, who is actually getting these jobs?</v>

94
00:10:32.327 --> 00:10:39.953
<v Daniel>Holding all other factors fixed, like occupation, industry, and county, the demographic profile is stark. They are significantly more educated.</v>

95
00:10:40.253 --> 00:10:40.774
<v Maya>How much more?</v>

96
00:10:41.214 --> 00:10:48.800
<v Daniel>Having an advanced degree makes a worker 2.9 percentage points more likely to be in new work compared to someone with just a high school diploma.</v>

97
00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.922
<v Maya>Okay, so highly educated. What else?</v>

98
00:10:52.282 --> 00:10:59.348
<v Daniel>They also tend to be younger, primarily under 30. They live in urban areas, and disproportionately they are white and female.</v>

99
00:10:59.752 --> 00:11:07.896
<v Maya>So it's mostly young, highly educated city dwellers capturing this new terrain. But are these jobs actually worth abandoning a traditional career path for?</v>

100
00:11:08.476 --> 00:11:12.898
<v Daniel>Oh, the financial payoff is highly lucrative, but only if it involves technology.</v>

101
00:11:13.478 --> 00:11:14.319
<v Maya>What's the difference in pay?</v>

102
00:11:15.079 --> 00:11:23.743
<v Daniel>Tech-linked new work pays a premium of about 8.3 percent within the exact same occupation. Non-tech new work pays effectively zero premium.</v>

103
00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:34.037
<v Maya>Wait, let's translate that into human terms. If you are making $60,000, having a newly invented tech skill bumps you up by about $5,000 overnight.</v>

104
00:11:34.298 --> 00:11:40.867
<v Daniel>Exactly. Compared to a peer in your exact same field, you earn more simply because your specific skill is rare.</v>

105
00:11:41.467 --> 00:11:44.408
<v Maya>And that 8.3% is the conservative estimate, right?</v>

106
00:11:44.508 --> 00:11:52.171
<v Daniel>Yeah, that's when we strictly control for demographics. The raw wage premium for TechLink New Work is actually closer to 15.6 log points.</v>

107
00:11:52.471 --> 00:11:57.992
<v Maya>Jeez. That's a massive earnings gap before we even filter out the demographic advantages.</v>

108
00:11:58.192 --> 00:11:58.713
<v Daniel>It really is.</v>

109
00:11:59.013 --> 00:12:05.635
<v Maya>But I'm looking at this data, which ends in 2023, and I have to ask the obvious question about selection bias here.</v>

110
00:12:06.035 --> 00:12:06.375
<v Daniel>Go ahead.</v>

111
00:12:06.976 --> 00:12:11.898
<v Maya>Couldn't this just be that hyper-ambitious, naturally talented people gravitate towards shiny new tech roles?</v>

112
00:12:12.419 --> 00:12:15.100
<v Daniel>Ah, the classic correlation versus causation problem.</v>

113
00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:15.661
<v Maya>Right.</v>

114
00:12:16.021 --> 00:12:23.605
<v Daniel>If an Ivy League graduate decides to become a machine learning engineer in 2018, they probably would have been a high earner no matter what they did.</v>

115
00:12:23.865 --> 00:12:24.926
<v Maya>It's a very valid point.</v>

116
00:12:25.526 --> 00:12:29.909
<v Daniel>How do we know it's the job paying a premium and not just the person being inherently high achieving?</v>

117
00:12:30.329 --> 00:12:31.830
<v Maya>Exactly. How do they control for that?</v>

118
00:12:32.325 --> 00:12:41.215
<v Daniel>Well, we can't entirely rule out selection bias because the modern American community survey data is cross-sectional. It's just a snapshot in time.</v>

119
00:12:41.456 --> 00:12:42.897
<v Maya>Okay, so it could just be the people.</v>

120
00:12:43.137 --> 00:12:50.226
<v Daniel>It could be. But the Otter Paper uncovers a mechanism that strongly argues against this just being about naturally talented people.</v>

121
00:12:50.546 --> 00:12:51.287
<v Maya>What's the mechanism?</v>

122
00:12:52.099 --> 00:12:56.247
<v Daniel>The premium is not permanent. It has a strict expiration date.</v>

123
00:12:56.347 --> 00:13:01.977
<v Maya>Here's where it gets really interesting. Expertise used to be a permanent foundation you built your entire career on.</v>

124
00:13:02.117 --> 00:13:02.518
<v Daniel>Not anymore.</v>

125
00:13:02.799 --> 00:13:05.043
<v Maya>Now, it's a lot more like a software subscription.</v>

126
00:13:05.397 --> 00:13:06.498
<v Daniel>That is the perfect analogy.</v>

127
00:13:06.518 --> 00:13:08.359
<v Maya>The premium fades with vintage.</v>

128
00:13:08.559 --> 00:13:16.183
<v Daniel>Exactly. If we look at the data, job titles introduced between 2000 and 2018 carry roughly a 4.7 log point premium.</v>

129
00:13:16.423 --> 00:13:16.763
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

130
00:13:16.943 --> 00:13:25.908
<v Daniel>But titles from the 1980s and 90s, they dropped down to a 1.6 premium. And titles introduced in the 1970s carry basically zero premium today.</v>

131
00:13:26.329 --> 00:13:27.089
<v Maya>It just vanishes.</v>

132
00:13:27.209 --> 00:13:31.591
<v Daniel>Completely. Meanwhile, the absolute newest TechLink titles boast a massive 9.1 log point premium.</v>

133
00:13:33.277 --> 00:13:35.458
<v Maya>This has to be terrifying for our university.</v>

134
00:13:35.758 --> 00:13:37.758
<v Daniel>Oh, I imagine it keeps curriculum directors up at night.</v>

135
00:13:37.938 --> 00:13:47.481
<v Maya>Right. It means if a student learns a highly specific coding language their freshman year, by the time they graduate, the financial payoff for that skill might have already started evaporating.</v>

136
00:13:47.561 --> 00:13:47.901
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

137
00:13:48.762 --> 00:13:50.562
<v Maya>Why does the value disappear so fast?</v>

138
00:13:51.282 --> 00:13:56.144
<v Daniel>Because of the diffusion of knowledge. When a new technology first arrives, very few people know how to use it.</v>

139
00:13:56.602 --> 00:13:58.383
<v Maya>So that expertise is incredibly scarce.</v>

140
00:13:58.603 --> 00:14:04.426
<v Daniel>Right. And employers have to pay a massive premium to attract the few people who have it. But over time, society adapts.</v>

141
00:14:04.906 --> 00:14:08.007
<v Maya>Universities build training programs. The software gets easier to use.</v>

142
00:14:08.287 --> 00:14:15.151
<v Daniel>Exactly. More people enter the field. The expertise is no longer scarce. So the wage premium just naturally fades back to zero.</v>

143
00:14:15.551 --> 00:14:22.834
<v Maya>So sustaining labor's share of the economic pie requires continual, relentless innovation and skill formation. You</v>

144
00:14:22.854 --> 00:14:24.435
<v Daniel>have to keep running just to stay in place.</v>

145
00:14:25.215 --> 00:14:28.376
<v Maya>But the data also shows a silver lining for the workers themselves, doesn't it?</v>

146
00:14:28.616 --> 00:14:35.778
<v Daniel>Yes, it does. The researchers linked individual census records from 1940 and 1950, tracking the exact same people over a decade.</v>

147
00:14:36.258 --> 00:14:37.079
<v Maya>And what did they find?</v>

148
00:14:37.779 --> 00:14:44.701
<v Daniel>They found that people who were doing new work in 1940 were nearly three times as likely to be doing new work again in 1950.</v>

149
00:14:45.241 --> 00:14:49.342
<v Maya>Wow. 19% versus, what, 7.2% for everyone else?</v>

150
00:14:49.482 --> 00:14:54.384
<v Daniel>Exactly. And they earned significantly more in 1950, even when controlling for their past earnings.</v>

151
00:14:54.915 --> 00:15:00.337
<v Maya>That strongly implies that once you learn how to acquire novel expertise, you develop this meta skill.</v>

152
00:15:00.637 --> 00:15:07.880
<v Daniel>Right. You get good at learning the next new thing. You learn how to jump from one fading software update to the next emerging one.</v>

153
00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:15.382
<v Maya>Okay. So if this highly paid tech linked new work is our best defense against automation, how do we actually get more of it?</v>

154
00:15:15.523 --> 00:15:16.723
<v Daniel>That's the million dollar question.</v>

155
00:15:16.951 --> 00:15:20.253
<v Maya>Does it just sprout up magically whenever the free market demands it?</v>

156
00:15:20.613 --> 00:15:26.696
<v Daniel>Not necessarily. Public policy actually plays a massive, often overlooked role in seeding new expertise.</v>

157
00:15:27.217 --> 00:15:27.917
<v Maya>We have data on that.</v>

158
00:15:28.177 --> 00:15:34.360
<v Daniel>We do. The Autor paper highlights a historical experiment analyzed by Guerin and Rothbaum regarding World War II.</v>

159
00:15:34.961 --> 00:15:36.201
<v Maya>Oh, the manufacturing plants.</v>

160
00:15:36.302 --> 00:15:46.187
<v Daniel>Yes. During the war, the U.S. government financed massive new manufacturing plants in specific, somewhat random counties across the country to build bomber parts and munitions.</v>

161
00:15:46.843 --> 00:15:53.272
<v Maya>This was a colossal injection of public investment into places that were often largely agricultural before the war, right?</v>

162
00:15:53.452 --> 00:15:53.893
<v Daniel>Entirely.</v>

163
00:15:54.414 --> 00:15:57.598
<v Maya>So what did these workers actually do that constituted new work?</v>

164
00:15:58.239 --> 00:16:04.007
<v Daniel>They had to learn precision machining. They were working with new alloys to microscopic tolerances.</v>

165
00:16:04.548 --> 00:16:07.750
<v Maya>Skills that simply did not exist in those local economies before the war.</v>

166
00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:19.919
<v Daniel>Exactly. And when researchers looked at those specific treated counties in 1950, long after the war ended, they found that the share of new work was about 0.35 percentage points higher than in control counties.</v>

167
00:16:20.759 --> 00:16:23.740
<v Maya>On the surface, a third of a percentage point doesn't sound very dramatic.</v>

168
00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:35.065
<v Daniel>Right. And to be intellectually honest about the limits of this study, that specific 0.35% bump in the new work share is relatively small compared to the massive overall earnings boost those plants created.</v>

169
00:16:35.445 --> 00:16:36.626
<v Maya>But the causal link is there.</v>

170
00:16:37.026 --> 00:16:48.031
<v Daniel>Undeniably. Public investments successfully seeded local new work. It created a demand shock that allowed incumbent workers, people already living there, to move into completely new specialized roles.</v>

171
00:16:48.831 --> 00:16:51.814
<v Maya>So policy can literally plant the seeds for new expertise.</v>

172
00:16:52.255 --> 00:16:53.015
<v Daniel>It absolutely can.</v>

173
00:16:53.576 --> 00:16:57.200
<v Maya>Which brings us to the most urgent question for anyone looking at the future of work today.</v>

174
00:16:57.620 --> 00:16:58.561
<v Daniel>Generative AI.</v>

175
00:16:58.761 --> 00:17:05.668
<v Maya>Generative AI. Because the auto data ends in 2023. It stops right before the explosion of chat GPT.</v>

176
00:17:05.949 --> 00:17:09.032
<v Daniel>Right. So we are essentially flying blind on the newest tech.</v>

177
00:17:09.445 --> 00:17:16.469
<v Maya>And we are looking at a technology that doesn't just automate physical tasks like a mechanical switchboard. It automates cognition.</v>

178
00:17:16.709 --> 00:17:16.950
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

179
00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:21.852
<v Maya>So is Gen AI going to create new work or is it just going to destroy it?</v>

180
00:17:22.153 --> 00:17:27.816
<v Daniel>For the earliest glimpses of that answer, we turn to a brand new 2026 paper by Humlum and Vestergaard.</v>

181
00:17:28.016 --> 00:17:29.217
<v Maya>This is the Danish study, right?</v>

182
00:17:29.317 --> 00:17:37.862
<v Daniel>Yeah. They conducted an incredibly detailed survey of Danish workers across 11 highly exposed occupations two years after ChatGPT launched.</v>

183
00:17:38.052 --> 00:17:41.074
<v Maya>And they linked those surveys to administrative tax and labor records.</v>

184
00:17:41.314 --> 00:17:43.415
<v Daniel>They did. It's incredibly robust data.</v>

185
00:17:43.595 --> 00:17:49.778
<v Maya>So what did they find? Did they find a massive spike in wages for AI users or a total collapse in hours for those who ignored it?</v>

186
00:17:50.119 --> 00:17:50.379
<v Daniel>Neither.</v>

187
00:17:50.719 --> 00:17:51.619
<v Maya>Wait, really? Neither?</v>

188
00:17:51.639 --> 00:17:55.461
<v Daniel>Neither. They found precise null effects on earnings and hours across the board.</v>

189
00:17:55.862 --> 00:18:00.544
<v Maya>Null effects. So wages haven't budged a single cent and nobody's hours have been cut.</v>

190
00:18:00.844 --> 00:18:07.468
<v Daniel>Exactly. They can confidently rule out any earnings effect larger than 2%. The surface of the water looks completely still.</v>

191
00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:13.004
<v Maya>But underneath that still water, how are the actual tasks shifting? Because it can't just be people writing clever prompts.</v>

192
00:18:13.444 --> 00:18:16.667
<v Daniel>No. Writing prompts is way too easy to be considered scarce expertise.</v>

193
00:18:16.727 --> 00:18:18.308
<v Maya>Right. So what are they doing?</v>

194
00:18:18.868 --> 00:18:32.739
<v Daniel>Well, you've hit the nail on the head. Underneath, the currents are moving rapidly. They found that 8% of chatbot users have already taken on entirely new tasks that did not exist in their roles two years ago.</v>

195
00:18:33.319 --> 00:18:34.320
<v Maya>8% is a solid chunk.</v>

196
00:18:34.881 --> 00:18:41.664
<v Daniel>And it gets bigger. In workplaces where employers actively push AI adoption, that number jumps to 17%. Wow.</v>

197
00:18:43.005 --> 00:18:46.127
<v Maya>So what does that new work actually look like in an office setting?</v>

198
00:18:46.727 --> 00:18:54.391
<v Daniel>It involves mass content generation, certainly. But more importantly, it involves AI oversight and complex integration.</v>

199
00:18:54.951 --> 00:18:57.732
<v Maya>What does AI integration mean for a regular worker?</v>

200
00:18:58.453 --> 00:19:04.476
<v Daniel>It means workers are having to completely restructure their company's legacy databases just to make them readable for the AI.</v>

201
00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:10.754
<v Maya>So they are building the infrastructure that allows the AI to function within the company's specific context.</v>

202
00:19:11.194 --> 00:19:19.180
<v Daniel>Exactly. Furthermore, the workers who adopt these tools are already beginning to transition into higher paying occupations where AI is more relevant.</v>

203
00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:23.903
<v Maya>So it's the very first sign of AI era new work forming in real time.</v>

204
00:19:24.183 --> 00:19:30.307
<v Daniel>Yes. We are seeing novel expertise develop on the ground before it ever officially shows up in the national pay data.</v>

205
00:19:31.048 --> 00:19:34.310
<v Maya>This highlights an incredible dual tension that you really need to understand.</v>

206
00:19:34.723 --> 00:19:35.363
<v Daniel>Let's lay it out.</v>

207
00:19:35.924 --> 00:19:44.450
<v Maya>First, we have the tech tension. Will generative AI create expertise demanding new work, just like past computers did? Or is gen AI fundamentally different? Right.</v>

208
00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:50.934
<v Daniel>Is it the first technology that can actually learn the new software itself, shortening the human wage premium to almost zero?</v>

209
00:19:51.174 --> 00:19:58.479
<v Maya>Exactly. If the AI can learn the new coding language faster than the human student, the premium for human expertise evaporates instantly.</v>

210
00:19:58.639 --> 00:19:59.800
<v Daniel>It's a very real possibility.</v>

211
00:20:00.393 --> 00:20:06.957
<v Maya>And second, we have the distributional tension. We know new work flows disproportionately to the young, the highly educated and the urban.</v>

212
00:20:07.317 --> 00:20:11.020
<v Daniel>While the displacement falls heavily on incumbent mid-career workers.</v>

213
00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:18.124
<v Maya>Just like the AT&T operators. Displacement and new work are happening at the exact same time, but they are happening to completely different people.</v>

214
00:20:18.525 --> 00:20:25.949
<v Daniel>And this is where all of this data lands squarely on the shoulders of higher education, especially in a diverse, complex environment like New York City.</v>

215
00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:28.391
<v Maya>So what does this all mean for universities?</v>

216
00:20:28.841 --> 00:20:39.272
<v Daniel>Well, if we refer back to that Lynn 2011 paper, we know that new work concentrates heavily in places that are dense in college graduates and have a massive variety of industries.</v>

217
00:20:39.733 --> 00:20:41.795
<v Maya>Which makes New York City a prime incubator.</v>

218
00:20:41.975 --> 00:20:51.525
<v Daniel>Exactly. The collisions of different industries create novel problems that require novel expertise. And universities are the engines where that new expertise is formed.</v>

219
00:20:51.872 --> 00:21:01.056
<v Maya>But look at the staggering challenge for a curriculum committee. Your mandate is to teach the skills for emerging job titles before those job titles even have official names. It's</v>

220
00:21:01.756 --> 00:21:03.057
<v Daniel>a nearly impossible timeline.</v>

221
00:21:03.637 --> 00:21:12.701
<v Maya>And because we know the wage premium for new work fades quickly as knowledge diffuses, universities cannot just teach a static set of technical skills anymore.</v>

222
00:21:13.021 --> 00:21:17.023
<v Daniel>Right. They have to teach the meta ability to continuously learn and adapt.</v>

223
00:21:17.737 --> 00:21:21.819
<v Maya>And they also have to figure out how to credential this new work at the speed of the market.</v>

224
00:21:22.239 --> 00:21:30.724
<v Daniel>You cannot wait four years to accredit a degree in, say, AI database integration if local employers need that scarce expertise tomorrow.</v>

225
00:21:30.944 --> 00:21:36.427
<v Maya>There is also a profound equity gap here. Think about the demographic reality of a public system like CUNY.</v>

226
00:21:36.927 --> 00:21:38.248
<v Daniel>Oh, the exposure gap is stark.</v>

227
00:21:38.448 --> 00:21:46.112
<v Maya>Right. Because you serve adult learners, first generation students and incumbent mid-career workers whose legacy tasks are actively being automated.</v>

228
00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:50.163
<v Daniel>That is the exact demographic profile most exposed to displacement.</v>

229
00:21:50.503 --> 00:21:57.948
<v Maya>Meanwhile, the data clearly shows that the lucrative new work premium is currently being captured by younger, already highly educated workers.</v>

230
00:21:58.208 --> 00:22:00.310
<v Daniel>So how does a public university close that gap?</v>

231
00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:01.991
<v Maya>By acting as the bridge.</v>

232
00:22:02.431 --> 00:22:11.058
<v Daniel>Exactly. Just like the World War II manufacturing plants seeded new work through intentional public investment, universities must aggressively partner with employers.</v>

233
00:22:11.318 --> 00:22:15.521
<v Maya>They need to advocate for public investments that intentionally seed new work locally.</v>

234
00:22:16.129 --> 00:22:23.653
<v Daniel>The goal has to be ensuring that vulnerable mid-career populations have a direct, rapidly credentialed bridge to access that new work premium.</v>

235
00:22:23.933 --> 00:22:26.615
<v Maya>Rather than being left to face the automation wave alone.</v>

236
00:22:26.975 --> 00:22:29.757
<v Daniel>Exactly. It's about intentional intervention.</v>

237
00:22:30.077 --> 00:22:35.820
<v Maya>Let's recap the hard evidence we've extracted today. We started by dismantling the lump of labor fallacy.</v>

238
00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:42.644
<v Daniel>Right. We saw that while displacement is a real threat, new work is the historical reinstatement mechanism.</v>

239
00:22:43.405 --> 00:22:49.147
<v Maya>We learned that new work isn't just a higher volume of tasks. It is driven by scarce, novel expertise.</v>

240
00:22:49.707 --> 00:22:55.268
<v Daniel>And that expertise pays a massive premium, around 8.3% for TechLink skills.</v>

241
00:22:55.288 --> 00:22:59.729
<v Maya>But that premium eventually fades like an old software update as society catches up.</v>

242
00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:06.491
<v Daniel>And finally, we saw that public investment and agile universities are the vital bridges to that premium.</v>

243
00:23:07.211 --> 00:23:09.572
<v Maya>It is a narrative of profound adaptation.</v>

244
00:23:10.264 --> 00:23:13.706
<v Daniel>But it requires aggressive intervention to ensure those benefits are shared.</v>

245
00:23:14.026 --> 00:23:18.609
<v Maya>Which leaves us with a final provocative thought for you to mull over as you explore this topic further.</v>

246
00:23:18.909 --> 00:23:19.390
<v Daniel>What's the thought?</v>

247
00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:34.839
<v Maya>Well, we know the wage premium for new work fades as knowledge diffuses. But if artificial intelligence accelerates the diffusion of knowledge, if it makes it incredibly easy for anyone to learn a new skill instantly, then that premium is going to fade faster than ever before.</v>

248
00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:35.940
<v Daniel>Oh, wow.</v>

249
00:23:36.764 --> 00:23:48.329
<v Maya>If human expertise expires faster than it takes to complete a bachelor's degree, does the traditional university model, selling a one-time, four-year credential, need to become a lifelong subscription?</v>

250
00:23:48.829 --> 00:23:50.430
<v Daniel>A lifelong subscription to learning.</v>

251
00:23:50.770 --> 00:24:03.695
<v Maya>Right. If expertise is no longer a permanent foundation you'll build a career on, but a constantly moving treadmill, perhaps higher education must become a constant, lifelong utility rather than a single life event.</v>

252
00:24:04.253 --> 00:24:09.786
<v Daniel>That is a fascinating paradigm shift to consider. Thank you for joining us in this analysis.</v>

253
00:24:10.528 --> 00:24:14.317
<v Maya>Thanks for diving deep with us today. Good luck putting this all together. Catch you next time.</v>
